course: dictionary
lesson: 4489
title: Strong feelings and the word hate
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To hate means to dislike someone or something very, very strongly. People also say «I hate peas» for small dislikes, but the word is strong, so choose it with care.
explain: Look at the words around hate. «Sam, who hates talking in front of people, spoke up in assembly.» Hates is a verb here, and the words tell you Sam really dislikes it. «Hateful comments about someone's skin colour or religion should be reported» shows that hate can hurt people badly.
explain: Hate can also be a noun, meaning a very strong feeling of dislike. «I'd hate to be late» does not mean you enjoy being late. It means you would not like to be late, or you are sorry to say it.
fact: Hate means to dislike someone or something very, very strongly.
fact: «I hate peas» is a casual way to say you really dislike something small.
fact: «I'd hate to…» means you would not like to do something, or you are sorry to say it.
fact: Hating people is harmful, and «I hate you» can hurt deeply even if you did not mean it.
fact: «Hate speech» and bullying because of who someone is are wrong, and hateful comments should be reported.
exercise: «After a month of walks they find they both hate the same teacher's jokes.» What does hate mean here? | To forget something quickly | *To dislike something very strongly | To be sorry about something | To love something a lot
exercise: «I'd hate to be late for the match.» What does the speaker mean? | They really enjoy being late | They are late every day | They hate the match | *They would not like to be late, and they are sorry to say it
exercise: Hate (noun) means what? | A kind of food | A happy word | *A very strong feeling of dislike | A game played in the park
test: «Careful with money» is a kind way to say someone is stingy, hating to spend or share money. What does stingy mean? | Very tired | Very happy | Very generous | *Hating to spend or share
test: Someone says «Hateful comments about someone's religion should be reported.» What should you do with hateful comments? | Ignore them and say nothing, because silence is safest | Join in to fit in | *Report them | Laugh so the person feels better
exam: Which word is the strongest way to show dislike? | Don't like very much | *Hate | Mildly not keen | Slightly bored
